== Date ==
This class is deprecated after Java 1.1
Works with longs (milliseconds after december 31st 1969)
It's a bad option because can't offer good presentation of dates and internationalization of time zone

== Calendar ==
Isn't deprecated, but obsolete, more used in legacy systems
Calendar is an abstract class, if you want to instantiate this, create an object of any of his subclasses (GregorianCalendar, BuddhistCalendar)

=== after Java 8 ===
+ Immutable
+ Don't need to parse everytime
+ MIN and MAX
+ You can do more things using referringClass.get(ChronoField.ENUM)

== LocalDate ==
+ No limit for dates
+ Use the pattern of the databases

== LocalTime ==
+ Works like LocalDate

== LocalDateTime ==
+ Works like the others above
+ Can be parsed with LocalDate.atTime(LocalTime or time) or LocalTime.atDate(LocalDate or date)